Well part of the problem is in the programming language. While I read the
book you mentioned or at least part of it, and I've seen old versions of the
game of life they don't really translate well into the programming languages
we have today.
The game of life is simple rules and all it generates is a pattern. Real
life itself is much more complicated with rules concerning everthing from
the 4 basic forces to non-quantum physics, to the biology of life on this
planet. While we may find a theory of everything as it relates to the main 4
forces that is hardly everything. And to make it more difficult there may be
a 5th force.

Now as to a game like nethack, somethings are simple, but I can't see any
way to have a set of rules produce the same effects. Lets use your example
of a towel. What rules do you write that will generate an object that you
can blind yourself with, clean hands, and face as those are things that it
makes sense for a towel to do. Now that may not be so hard to do, and you
could say that the current code does just that. But those same simple rules
also have to be able to generate a pick ax, a magic marker and the rest of
the tool set. Now apply that to weapons, armor, and all the rest.

Now if you could write a code that could do all this with a simple set of
rules that would be great, but you also want it to do things not included in
the current game. We can take any YANI you see here that you like for
examples. Lets take a popular one according to the faq. Used potions leave
empty bottles. Which from my understanding is not in the game as there isn't
a feasable way to code it. Also those rules may generate lots of pure junk.
Say a ring which you could apply to to move one space to the left but it
costs you 30 food to do. Nobody would use such an item, and on the meta
level no wizard with the ability to create such a ring would but it would
turn up.

Also consider that many of the things in nethack make sense from a human
perspective, in paticular a western perspective. Using a towel to wipe your
face isn't an act of genius but who would think that a dwarven mithril could
be applied to cure blindness? Also consider ballance, if the rules generate
an artifact that does silver damage and doubles and is generated at +15 with
a bonus to ac who would use anything else. Now consider that this artifact
is a mace called Halerath. What do you put in the information file for it.

But in the end it boils down to programming capability. We have no
programming language to my knowledge that can generate such a thing as
nethack from a few simple rules.

Now for a half idea, other roguelikes have random artifacts. By other
roguelikes I mean some variants of angband. Since there are much more
abilities for artifacts to have and a great deal more items a few simple
rules could generate artifacts. But once again a great deal of nethacks
flavor comes from sources we have knowledge of. And how would you like a
chaotic dagger named excaliber with a few random abilities.



